"Textbook: OpenStax <em>Astronomy 2e</em>, §{#} (the brightness of stars and magnitudes), §{#} to §{#} (a census of the stars) and §{#} (distances to stars). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/astronomy-2e.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> five modes, chosen above the picture. Inverse square: The first square, Twice as far, Three times as far, The Sun from Jupiter. Two stars: {#} times as luminous, {#} times as far; {#} times as luminous, equally bright; A giant and a red dwarf; Twins at different distances. Magnitudes: After sunset; From town, magnitude {#}; A dark field, magnitude {#}; Vega and a magnitude {#} star. Absolute magnitude: The Sun, Sirius, Deneb, Proxima Centauri, m − M = {#}. Census: Count the neighbors, Only what the eye sees, Why the sky lies. This worksheet uses <strong>Two stars</strong>, <strong>Absolute magnitude</strong> and <strong>Census</strong>.",
"Two stars sets each star’s luminosity (in units of the Sun’s, L☉) and distance (in parsecs, pc; {#} pc = {#} light years) and shows how bright each looks.",
"Absolute magnitude places a star on a distance line; <strong>Play</strong> carries it to {#} pc, where its apparent magnitude equals its absolute magnitude.",
"Census maps every star in the HYG catalog within {#} pc of the Sun, and counts those inside the <strong>Survey radius</strong> by spectral class. Click the Survey radius readout to type a radius. The check box <strong>Show only the stars you can see without a telescope</strong> keeps only stars of magnitude {#} or brighter.",
"Record reading (Census mode only) stores the survey radius and the number of stars inside it: all of them, the red dwarfs (class M) and those more luminous than the Sun. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the first data table. Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"The data: the HYG star database version {#} (D. Nash), from the Hipparcos, Yale Bright Star and Gliese catalogs. Luminosities are in the V band, the light the eye sees best.",
"Use the inverse square law and the magnitude scale to compare stars, then count the stars near the Sun and find out how many there are in each cubic parsec, and how many of them you could ever see.",
"<strong>Luminosity and brightness.</strong> A star’s luminosity \\(L\\) is the light it gives off each second. How bright it looks from here, its brightness \\(b\\), is the light that reaches each square meter of Earth. The light spreads out over a sphere whose area grows as the square of the distance \\(d\\), so",
"\\[b \\propto \\frac{L}{d^2}.\\]",
"Twice as far means a quarter as bright. Comparing two stars A and B, \\(b_A/b_B = (L_A/L_B)(d_B/d_A)^2\\).",
"<strong>Magnitudes.</strong> Astronomers rank brightness with magnitudes, a scale that runs backward: a bigger magnitude is a fainter star. Five magnitudes are a factor of exactly {#} in brightness, so one magnitude is a factor of \\(100^{1/5} = 2.512\\). A star of magnitude {#} is {#} times brighter than one of magnitude {#}, the faintest you can see on a dark night.",
"<strong>Absolute magnitude.</strong> The magnitude a star seems to have, \\(m\\), depends on its distance. Its <strong>absolute magnitude</strong> \\(M\\) is the magnitude it would have at {#} pc. Each factor of {#} in distance is a factor of {#} in brightness, which is {#} magnitudes. So a star at {#} pc has \\(m - M = 0\\), at {#} pc \\(m - M = 5\\), at {#} pc \\(m - M = 10\\), and at {#} pc \\(m - M = 15\\).",
"<strong>Counting stars.</strong> If stars were spread evenly through space, with \\(n\\) stars in each cubic parsec, the number inside a sphere of radius \\(r\\) around the Sun would be",
"\\[N = n \\times \\tfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3,\\]",
"so \\(N\\) graphed against \\(r^3\\) is a straight line through the origin with slope \\(\\tfrac43\\pi n\\). Doubling the radius takes in {#} times the volume and should take in {#} times the stars.",
"Jupiter is {#} AU from the Sun. How bright does the Sun look from Jupiter, as a fraction of how bright it looks from Earth ({#} AU)?",
"Star A is {#} times as luminous as star B but {#} times as far away. Which looks brighter, and by what factor?",
"Which looks brighter, a star of magnitude {#} or one of magnitude {#}, and by what factor? A star of magnitude {#} or one of magnitude {#}?",
"On a dark night you can see a few thousand stars. Now think of every star within {#} pc of the Sun, our nearest neighbors. Guess what fraction of them you could see with your eyes alone, and whether most of them are more luminous or less luminous than the Sun. Write your guesses, with a reason, before you open the demo.",
